In the conversation, James inquired about estimating a benefit-cost ratio (BCR) from an economic internal rate of return (IRR). Arachne confirmed that a BCR could be derived using cash flow details and a discount rate. James then specified assumptions: costs in year 0 and constant benefits from year 1. Arachne provided a formula to calculate BCR based on these assumptions and indicated that if only IRR is known, BCR equals 1 when the discount rate is equal to IRR. James further sought clarification on the relationship between IRR and BCR when the discount rate differs from IRR. Arachne explained that if the discount rate is lower than IRR, BCR is greater than 1; if equal, BCR is 1; and if the discount rate is higher than IRR, BCR is less than 1, summarizing their interplay.
